WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.880
Now let's discuss a special thing for our quicksort.

00:02.880 --> 00:06.280
The worst case time complexity of a quicksort is when it comes.

00:06.280 --> 00:11.280
Let's look at this lecture only when the time complexity is already clear.

00:11.280 --> 00:14.280
We already know about space complexity.

00:14.280 --> 00:18.680
Otherwise, we can skip this lecture and look at it after the time complexity chapter.

00:18.680 --> 00:25.280
The worst case time complexity comes in a quicksort when my entire array is sorted in ascending order or descending order.

00:25.280 --> 00:30.280
And this is when my pivot element is always the smallest element or the largest element.

00:30.280 --> 00:33.280
How? Let's assume that my array looks like this.

00:33.280 --> 00:36.280
1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

00:36.280 --> 00:40.280
Now what will happen if I take my pivot element as the last element?

00:40.280 --> 00:47.280
If my pivot element is always the last element, then my pivot element will always be the largest element of the array.

00:47.280 --> 00:53.280
Why? Because the last element sorted in ascending order is always the largest of the array.

00:53.280 --> 00:57.280
So if I call a quicksort here, then this partition will be divided in two parts.

00:57.280 --> 01:01.280
If partition is divided through 5, then what will happen before this?

01:01.280 --> 01:04.280
Before this, I will have 1, 2, 3, and then 4.

01:04.280 --> 01:06.280
And in the end, I will have empty.

01:06.280 --> 01:08.280
Again, the pivot will be 4.

01:08.280 --> 01:10.280
If it is 4, then it will be divided in two parts.

01:10.280 --> 01:11.280
In the middle, it will be 4.

01:11.280 --> 01:12.280
And here, it will be empty.

01:12.280 --> 01:14.280
1, 2, and 3.

01:14.280 --> 01:16.280
It will be empty here.

01:16.280 --> 01:18.280
Now again, it will be 3 as the pivot.

01:18.280 --> 01:20.280
If it is 3 as the pivot, then it will be 3 in the middle.

01:20.280 --> 01:22.280
This will also be divided in two parts.

01:22.280 --> 01:24.280
In the first part, I will have 1 and 2.

01:24.280 --> 01:26.280
And in the second part, it will be empty.

01:26.280 --> 01:28.280
After this, again, the pivot will choose 2 here.

01:28.280 --> 01:32.280
If I have two around, then this will come empty.

01:32.280 --> 01:34.280
And here, I will have 1.

01:34.280 --> 01:36.280
So what happened every time?

01:36.280 --> 01:38.280
Every time, my pivot element is the last element.

01:38.280 --> 01:40.280
That is, the largest element.

01:40.280 --> 01:42.280
So every time, the size of my array.

01:42.280 --> 01:45.280
Can I say that if I consider this as a recursion?

01:45.280 --> 01:48.280
So what is the size of my array on my first level?

01:48.280 --> 01:50.280
How many times do I have to reverse?

01:50.280 --> 01:52.280
How many times will my loop run in the partition?

01:52.280 --> 01:54.280
Here, I am talking about the partition function.

01:54.280 --> 01:56.280
In the partition function,

01:56.280 --> 01:59.280
my loop will run N times.

01:59.280 --> 02:01.280
At this level, in the partition,

02:01.280 --> 02:04.280
my time complexity will come N minus 1.

02:04.280 --> 02:07.280
At this level, my time complexity will come N minus 2.

02:07.280 --> 02:09.280
At this level, N minus 3 will come.

02:09.280 --> 02:11.280
At this level, N minus 4 will come.

02:11.280 --> 02:13.280
And in this way, it will go up to 1.

02:13.280 --> 02:15.280
So can I say that at one level of the recursion,

02:15.280 --> 02:17.280
the time complexity is N.

02:17.280 --> 02:19.280
Then there is N minus 1, then there is N minus 2,

02:19.280 --> 02:21.280
then there is N minus 3.

02:21.280 --> 02:23.280
And in my total time complexity,

02:23.280 --> 02:25.280
what do I do? I add them all.

02:25.280 --> 02:27.280
So N plus N minus 1,

02:27.280 --> 02:29.280
plus N minus 2,

02:29.280 --> 02:31.280
plus N minus 3.

02:31.280 --> 02:33.280
In this way, if I add 3, plus 2, plus 1,

02:33.280 --> 02:35.280
to all of them.

02:35.280 --> 02:37.280
Can I say that I have an arithmetic progression here?

02:37.280 --> 02:39.280
Can I say it in reverse?

02:39.280 --> 02:41.280
1 plus 2, plus 3, plus 4.

02:41.280 --> 02:43.280
And up to where? Up to N.

02:43.280 --> 02:45.280
So what is its sum?

02:45.280 --> 02:47.280
N plus 1 by 2.

02:47.280 --> 02:48.280
What is this?

02:48.280 --> 02:50.280
N square plus N by 2.

02:50.280 --> 02:52.280
And I can say this

02:52.280 --> 02:54.280
that it will be big of

02:54.280 --> 02:56.280
N square.

02:56.280 --> 02:57.280
So when will be the worst case?

02:57.280 --> 02:58.280
The worst case will be

02:58.280 --> 03:00.280
when in my ascending order,

03:00.280 --> 03:02.280
my pivot element is always the largest element.

03:02.280 --> 03:03.280
In that case,

03:03.280 --> 03:04.280
every single level will increase

03:04.280 --> 03:06.280
the time complexity.

03:06.280 --> 03:08.280
From which the final worst-case time complexity

03:08.280 --> 03:09.280
will be equal to N square.

03:09.280 --> 03:10.280
And in the same case,

03:10.280 --> 03:12.280
if it is sorted in descending order,

03:12.280 --> 03:14.280
i.e. if this area is in reverse,

03:14.280 --> 03:16.280
then it is always the smallest element.

03:16.280 --> 03:17.280
So in that case,

03:17.280 --> 03:19.280
the same N square time complexity goes.

03:19.280 --> 03:20.280
Now the question is

03:20.280 --> 03:22.280
that the worst-case time complexity of Quixote

03:22.280 --> 03:24.280
is asked in actual interviews.

03:24.280 --> 03:26.280
So you should already know this.

03:26.280 --> 03:28.280
In fact, it is very important to remember this.

03:28.280 --> 03:31.280
Make a note that the worst-case time complexity

03:31.280 --> 03:34.280
comes into my Quixote when my pivot element

03:34.280 --> 03:37.280
is always the smallest element

03:37.280 --> 03:40.280
or always the largest element.

